VERDICT: NO — HONEST-NEGATIVE. Do not build B4.
  B4's primary mechanism is ALREADY implemented, its remaining sub-mechanism is
  structurally inapplicable to the production prefill path, and it targets a
  component (attention) that is NOT the documented prefill bottleneck (weight
  dequant). Effort to build anyway = multi-week FlashAttention rewrite of 2 head
  shapes × 2 exactness modes, to chase a non-bottleneck. Not worth it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q1. HOW DOES PREFILL ATTENTION WORK TODAY? (naive [NxN]-to-HBM vs fused on-chip)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING: Every prefill/decode attention kernel is ALREADY FUSED / online-in-
shared-memory. The score row lives in on-chip shared memory and QK→softmax→·V is
a single kernel. NO kernel materializes an [N×N] (or [N×window]) score matrix to
HBM, and there is NO separate softmax kernel. B4's headline win is pre-captured.

Batched cold-prefill kernel (the only path with a query batch to tile):
  k_attn                          cuda_forward.cu:968-1002
    - launch: cuda_forward.cu:4960  k_attn<<<n*nh, bd, n*sizeof(float), st>>>
    - dynamic shared = n floats  = the score row `sc[]` (on-chip), line 971
    - line 981: sc[s] = acc*ascale;         (scores → SHARED MEM, not HBM)
    - line 985-992: row-max + exp-sum over sc[] in shared mem (fused softmax)
    - line 995-1001: acc += sc[s]*V[...]     (·V reads scores from shared mem)
  => single fused pass, zero HBM score traffic. This is a "half-flash" kernel.

Production per-token prefill kernels (gemma4_kv_prefill → g4_kv_step, one token
per step; single-query GQA over the resident KV cache), dispatch at :4475-4511:
  k_attn_decode_ring_bx   :716   (40 SWA layers, byte-exact, ring)   launch :4483
  k_attn_decode_ring      :670   (40 SWA layers, float, ring)        launch :4486
  k_attn_decode_win_bx    :556   (global/full ctx, byte-exact)       launch :4503
  k_attn_decode_win_dyn   :2346  (global/full ctx, float)            launch :4510
  All: dynamic shared = wl|ctx * sizeof(...) = score row in shared mem; fused
  QK→softmax→·V; scores NEVER written to HBM.

NEGATIVE CONTROL (the only HBM `scores` buffers exist, and are NOT attention):
  k_qk_scores :838, dscores cudaMalloc :3067, launch :3560, k_topb_dev :892
    = XBAR P3.2-b ARM shadow-ROUTER recall-set SELECTION (a diagnostic/router
    that scores QK to pick a sparse key set), NOT the attention output path.
  dg_k_softmax_rows :6931 / :7444 = DiffusionGemma per-row VOCAB softmax, not attn.

EVIDENCE CLASS: an online/fused running-max+running-sum over a shared-memory
score row = ALREADY TILED (FlashAttention-class on the score axis). The naive
signature B4 assumes (materialized scores buffer + separate softmax kernel) is
ABSENT from the prefill path.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q2. WHAT IS THE ACTUAL PREFILL BOTTLENECK? (attention vs FFN/weight GEMMs)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING: The documented prefill bottleneck is the WEIGHT-GEMM f32 dequant
bandwidth, NOT attention. B4 touches only attention and cannot move it.

  cuda_forward.cu:1409-1415 (verbatim): the batched prefill matmul
  "gemm_w_lift → k_dequant_arena_q4b, which MATERIALIZES the whole OK_Q4B weight
   as f32 into scratch [out×in] every call (7 matmuls × 48 layers) — the
   bandwidth cost center of the ~98s cold n=1765 prefill."
  The shipped prefill speed levers are ALL weight-side, never attention:
    - SP_KV_PREFILL_DP4A: packed int4 GEMM, "8× less bandwidth"      :1409-1415, :4855
    - batched prefill: amortize weight read across N tokens          :4762-4775
  Corroborated by MEMORY ADR-010/011/012: whole-machine perf is bandwidth-bound;
  the prefill STALL was fixed by arming the SWA ring (PK2-T1), not by attention.

  Traffic model (N=1765 = documented cold prefill; SW≈1024; upper bound, no L2):
    Weight dequant, BATCHED path (once/prefill):  ~96 GB f32  (~6.4 GB packed dp4a)
    Weight dequant, PER-TOKEN path (×N, PRODUCTION): N×~6.4–48 GB = ~11–85 TB
    Attention KV reads (batched, upper bound):    ~1.05 TB total
       global 8 layers : 65,536·N² B ≈ 204 GB (≈20%)
       SWA   40 layers : 8·(N·SW − SW²/2)·2048·40 B ≈ 843 GB (≈80%)
  In the PRODUCTION per-token path weight dequant dominates attention by ~1–2
  orders of magnitude. In the batched path the attention UPPER BOUND exceeds
  weights, BUT (a) that path is non-production (declines under persist-KV, Q3)
  and (b) the upper bound collapses under L2 (Q4).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q3. BYTE-EXACT ENVELOPE OF PREFILL ATTENTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FINDING: Prefill IS byte-exact-capable, but only via the PER-TOKEN path.
  - Per-token prefill, bx_on (SP_BYTEEXACT): exact-integer dual-prime Garner dot
    + FB30 integer softmax, reduction-order-immune —
      SWA layers    → k_attn_decode_ring_bx  :716 (launch :4483)
      global layers → k_attn_decode_win_bx   :556 (launch :4503)
    (bx chunked-fold detail :575-599, :632-651; G-BX-ATTN-FAST.)
  - Batched cold prefill (k_attn) is FLOAT ONLY — no bx twin. Comment :4769-4774:
    "FLOAT attention ⇒ K/V differ from the bx per-token path ... Bit-parity is
     NOT claimed; coherence + no-corruption + speed is the gate."
  IMPLICATION FOR B4: an exact IO-aware TILED kernel would have to reproduce the
  FB30 integer softmax as a STREAMING online-softmax (running max/sum) in the
  exact-integer domain while staying bit-identical to the CPU oracle. The current
  exact kernels do a two-phase max-then-exp over the FULL shared-mem row; making
  that streaming-yet-bit-identical is a new, non-trivial gate — not a free port.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Q4. SWA-vs-GLOBAL TRAFFIC SPLIT (bounds B4's max possible win)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Geometry (cuda_forward.cu:1687-1688, 1915-1916; arm_geom_test.c:43):
  48 layers, period 6, kvfs=48 (no KV sharing).
  Global (8 layers, L%6==5): head_dim 512, n_head 4, n_head_kv 1 → QD 2048, KVD 512,
    win = -1 (FULL CAUSAL, O(N²)).
  SWA (40 layers): head_dim 256, n_head 8, n_head_kv 2 → QD 2048, KVD 512,
    win = SW ≈ 1024 (O(N·window)).

Attention KV HBM upper bound at N=1765, SW=1024 (see Q2): SWA ≈ 80%, global ≈ 20%.
So the O(N²) term B4 could actually shrink (globals) is only ~1/5 of attention
traffic, and even that is only ~200 GB vs the ~11–85 TB production weight cost.

L2 EROSION (RTX 2060, 3 MB L2 — MEMORY reference_host_gpu_2060_12gb):
  SWA per-kv-head window working set = SW·head_dim·4·2(K,V) = 1024·256·4·2 = 2 MB
    < 3 MB L2  ⇒ SWA KV re-reads across adjacent query blocks are L2-SERVED, real
    HBM ≪ upper bound. FlashAttention's Θ(N²)→Θ(N) win is largely already realized
    by the L2 for the 40 SWA layers.
  Global per-kv-head causal set at N=1765 = 1765·512·4·2 = 7.2 MB > 3 MB ⇒ partial
    L2 reuse; some real HBM headroom, but only on 8 layers / ~20% of attn traffic.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY NO (summary of the four independent kill-shots)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. B4's headline mechanism ("keep the score matrix on-chip") is ALREADY DONE in
   every attention kernel (scores in shared mem; fused QK→softmax→·V). Zero
   headroom. There is NO [N×N]-to-HBM naive path to replace.  (:968, :510, :556)
2. Documented prefill bottleneck = WEIGHT f32 dequant bandwidth, not attention.
   B4 only touches attention.  (:1409-1415; ADR-010/011/012)
3. Production prefill is PER-TOKEN (persist-KV declines the batched path,
   :4808-4810), so there is NO query batch to tile — FlashAttention query-tiling
   is structurally inapplicable to the shipped path. The only place a query batch
   exists (batched cold prefill) is opt-in / non-production and FLOAT-only.
4. 40/48 layers are SWA (O(N·window), window 2 MB fits the 2060's 3 MB L2), so KV
   re-reads are already L2-served. Only 8 global layers are O(N²) (~20% of attn
   traffic, partial L2). Max theoretical win is small and on a non-bottleneck.

The one genuinely-real gap — batched k_attn re-reads KV per query head instead of
reusing a KV tile across a query tile — is (a) off the production path, (b) mostly
absorbed by L2 for the 40 SWA layers, and (c) dwarfed by the weight-dequant cost
that IS the bound. Tiling it would not move served prefill wall-clock.

EFFORT IF BUILT ANYWAY: NOT a small kernel tweak. A true exact IO-aware
FlashAttention would need TWO head shapes (global full-causal hd=512; SWA windowed
hd=256) × TWO exactness modes (float + dual-prime/Garner byte-exact with a NEW
streaming-online-softmax-in-integers bit-exact gate) = a multi-week rewrite. The
byte-exact streaming softmax gate alone is novel work. Recommend: DO NOT BUILD.
Redirect any prefill-perf effort to the weight-dequant lane (packed dp4a batched
GEMM already exists behind SP_KV_PREFILL_DP4A; that is where prefill bandwidth
lives).
